For EACH covered rule, judge the case against these criteria. A criterion that does not apply to a rule (for
example recovery, when the rule's refusal tells the agent nothing to do instead) is satisfied, and you say why.

1. REFUSAL AND PERMIT. The case contains a scenario the rule refuses AND a scenario it permits, each asserted
   on the rule's decision in the events (the rule named, the outcome "refused" / "permitted" / "passed").
   A rule with only a refusal proves it blocks everything; with only a permit, nothing.
2. REASON. Every asserted refusal also asserts its reason, and the reason it asserts is the rule's ACTUAL
   reason (what the rule's YAML, script or judge template refuses for), not an incidental word that any
   refusal from that rule would carry, such as the rule's name.
3. RECOVERY. Where the rule's refusal tells the agent what to do instead (its reason, README or script says
   "do X"), the case shows the agent doing X after the refusal and the rule then permitting. Where the
   rule's refusal tells the agent nothing to do, no recovery is needed.
4. BOUNDARY. The permit sits close to the rule's boundary: the nearest legitimate neighbour of the refused
   input (the same shape with the one offending thing fixed, or just outside the rule's `match`). A permit of
   something obviously unrelated (an empty commit, an unrelated file) does not count.
5. MOCKED JUDGES DECIDE. Where the rule has a judge check and the case mocks it (SR_CHECKS_JUDGE_MOCKS), the
   mock script decides from its stdin (the rendered prompt and payload) and returns different verdicts for
   the refused and the permitted scenario. A mock that returns one fixed answer, or ignores its input, proves
   nothing about the rule.
6. NAMED CASES. Every scenario the rule's README (or the rule's own comments) names as one it handles, as a
   refusal or as an allowed exception, and that this case claims to cover (by its folder name or its own
   comments), is really exercised by the case. A case that claims "the exception for X" but never writes X
   fails. A README scenario that this case does not claim is another case's business: do not fail for it.

Cite evidence for EVERY criterion of EVERY rule: the exact text from the case or the rule you rely on, in
quotes, with its file. A criterion you cannot support with a quote you found in the material fails.

Be conservative the other way too: report a failure only when you can point at specific text (or its
absence, after reading the whole case) and name the criterion it breaks. Do not add criteria of your own.

When the case is NOT rigorous, your reasoning MUST begin with the words `RULE TEST NOT RIGOROUS:` and then name,
in concrete sentences, the case, the rule, the criterion (REFUSAL AND PERMIT, REASON, RECOVERY, BOUNDARY,
MOCKED JUDGES DECIDE or NAMED CASES) and the quoted text or missing scenario, so the agent can fix the case.
When every criterion holds for every rule, it passes.
